            "full_citation": "Dallman v. Ritter, 225 P.3d 610, 620 (Colo. 2010)",
            "antecedent": null,
            "pin": "620",
            "flags": []
          },
          "children": [],
          "proposition": "33.The  six elements  a court must  consider  are:  (1) whether  there  is a reasonable\n     probability   of success   on the  merits;   (2) whether   there   is a danger   of real,  immediate,\n    and irreparable  injury which may be prevented  by injunctive  relief; (3) whether  there  is a\n     plain, speedy,  and adequate  remedy  at law; (4) whether  the granting of a preliminary\n     injunction   will disserve   the  public   interest;   (5) whether   the  balance   of equities   favors\n     the  injunction;   and  (6) whether   the  injunction   will preserve   the  status  quo  pending  a\n      trial on the  merits.   /d.;",
          "propositionSpan": [
            20539,
            21178
